+---
+id: dl-002-pass-the-current-time-into-the-domain-instead-of-reading-the-clock
+type: decision-log
+title: "Pass the current time into the domain instead of reading the clock"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Tasks needed `createdAt` and `updatedAt` timestamps, set when a task is added and moved on every
+change (complete, reopen, rename, tag, untag). `r3-no-clock-or-randomness` forbids `src/domain/` from
+reading the clock (`Date.now()`, `new Date()` with no arguments) anywhere, including `list.ts`, which
+is where every mutating `TaskList` method lives.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Every `TaskList` method that records a new point in time (`add`, `complete`, `reopen`, `rename`,
+  `tag`, `untag`) takes an extra, required parameter: `now: string`, an ISO-8601 date-time string.
+  The caller (the app, or a test) supplies it; the domain never generates it.
+- `src/domain/task.ts`'s corresponding functions (`createTask`, `completeTask`, `reopenTask`,
+  `renameTask`, `addTag`, `removeTag`) take the same `now` parameter and use it as-is for `createdAt`
+  and/or `updatedAt`.
+- `get`, `find`, `list`, `openCount` and `remove` are untouched: none of them records a new point in
+  time, so none needs `now`.
+- This mirrors the existing decision for task ids (`dl-001`): the one place that would otherwise reach
+  for a forbidden primitive instead receives it as an argument, so the domain stays deterministic and
+  easy to test with fixed timestamps.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Every caller of `add`/`complete`/`reopen`/`rename`/`tag`/`untag` must now pass the current time;
+  this is a breaking change to `TaskList`'s method signatures (the app's call sites need updating,
+  outside this package).
+- Tests pass fixed ISO strings instead of the real clock, which keeps them deterministic per the
+  Testing directive, and makes `updatedAt` assertions exact rather than approximate.
+- A `complete`/`reopen` call that fails (wrong status) returns an error and does not store a new task,
+  so `updatedAt` is left untouched on failure, which is the behaviour the new tests check.
